Two follow-ups from the same review pass that produced ac556dd, both found by re-reading the code rather than by running it.

git-notes-grep.sh's match loop threw away the note content it had just fetched via `git show "$note_blob"`, keeping only the timestamp and commit sha for sorting, then re-fetched the identical content a second time via `git notes show "$commit_sha"` in the print loop. Fixed by carrying the content through in a parallel `match_note` array alongside `match_ts`/`match_sha`, built at the same index in the same loop iteration. Used plain indexed arrays, not `declare -A`, for the same bash-3.2-on-macOS reason mapfile and paste were avoided on 8af5907 -- associative arrays are also a bash-4+-only feature.

Sorting: `git notes list` order is the note tree's internal order, not commit date order, so newest-first still needs an explicit sort. Rather than sort strings (which would require embedding multi-line note content inline and getting the field-splitting right), build a small "timestamp index" side table, sort just that, then use the sorted indices to pull from the parallel arrays. Keeps the sort key simple (two fields, no multi-line content anywhere near `sort`) while still avoiding the second fetch.

Separately: the previous fix (escaping regex metacharacters so keywords match literally) changed real, observable behavior, but neither README.md nor SKILL.md's description of git-notes-grep.sh was updated to say so -- both still described the old "OR'd together" search without mentioning literal matching. Caught in review, not by anyone hitting it. Added a parenthetical to both usage sections rather than a separate caveat, since it's a one-clause fact about how the search works, not a gotcha that needs its own callout.
